A||||6||rodmcneill||Front fascia removal?||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||10-29-2001||10:10 PM||||I got into a little accident today and I was wondering what the procedure for pulling the front fascia was. Where are all the bolts located and can I reuse all of the hardware-clips. I have not done any body work on a Fiero yet so this should be interesting. It is a 86GT. Thanks guys.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cheever3000||10-29-2001||10:20 PM||||First-timers may curse & spit like I did, but the next time is a breeze.  The worst part is the dozen or so screws underneath, because if your car has normal road-dirt, it will fall in your face, so wear safety glasses under there.  Other than that, just follow the contours all around, removing screws.  When you start to pull it off, it will feel real flexible and you may fear breaking it, but don't worry cause they can take a lot of bending & flexing.<P>Where's the pics?!<P>Oh yeah, there's a metal strip under the hood across from one side to the other, just inside -- about 7 screws there.  You'll have to take some screws out of the front wheelwell, and remove the marker lights.  Don't forget to unplug the turn signals.  There will be some bigger screws under the hood on the fenders above the front wheels, 3 or 4 on each side.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000002||Hartz||10-29-2001||10:23 PM||||I just did this today when I swapped fascias on my '84.  You need to remove the side marker lights, and there are some bolts under there.  There are some bolts in the front wheel well, and a bunch across the top of the fascia (I removed the hood to make it easier to get at them).  Then there are some plastic fasteners along the bottom of the fascia (at least there was on my '84 - your car may be different).  If you are careful, you can pull them out with a pair of pliers, and reuse them.  Lastly, there are some braces underneath the fascia, near the horns, that need to be unbolted.  I think the whole job took me less than an hour, and was really quite straight forward.  <P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||10-29-2001||11:21 PM||||Thanks for the replies. Today on a on ramp the lady in front of me decided to stop after she appeared to be going, I looked back to make sure it was still clear and accelerated,imagine my surprise when I looked in front and she is still sitting there! The front fascia got a little rip and scuffs, it could have been worse, no one was hurt. It has been about 25 years since my last "fender bender". It was almost the same thing, lady driver goes through the yellow and then decides to stop about 30 feet further up. I would rather deal with a more aggressive driver than an indecisive one. The only thing is when you rear end someone it is usually your fault.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hartz||10-30-2001||08:38 AM||||Ah, sorry to hear of it.  But, seriously, getting the front fascia off is a piece of cake.  If you have any problems, just shoot me an e-mail, and I'd be happy to walk you through it.<P>Good luck,<BR>Hartz<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Back On Holiday||10-31-2001||07:19 AM||||on my 86se the bolts on the front/top are T30 bolts, and they're too tight to pull off with a T30 Screwdriver, get a socket.  had to buy that socket for that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hartz||10-31-2001||09:00 AM||||The T-30 bolts are actually attaching the bracing, which is attached to the fascia using the good old 7mm screws.  If you've taken the hood off, you can just undo the 7mm screws, and then carefully pry the fascia up and over the bracing.  I liked this method as I didn't have to worry so much about the rubber hood adjusters in the corners.<P>Just a thought.<P>Hartz||||reg||1
